{"chunk_id": "48dae4d7dd8b", "text": "Victor Frankenstein throws himself obsessively into natural philosophy at Ingolstadt, guided by the contrasting professors Krempe and Waldman. His fascination narrows toward the ultimate mystery: the origin of life. To understand it, he studies anatomy and the decay of bodies, haunting vaults and charnel-houses until a breakthrough arrives\u2014he discovers the secret of generating life and animating lifeless matter. Rather than revealing the method, Victor warns that such knowledge brings ruin. Consumed by ambition, he decides to construct a human being, enlarging the design to a gigantic eight-foot form for practical reasons. Working alone in a hidden workshop, gathering bones and materials from dissecting rooms and slaughterhouses, he becomes physically and mentally degraded. Nature, family, and morality fade as his feverish labor drives him toward completing his creation.\nVictor obsessively studies anatomy and decay, discovers the secret of animating life, and begins constructing a gigantic human being from corpse parts.\nThemes: dangerous pursuit of knowledge, creation and hubris, life and death, scientific obsession, isolation from family", "vector": [0.00788510125130415, -0.008770770393311977, 0.014263242483139038, -0.013721264898777008, -0.01325860247015953, 0.015519041568040848, -0.026437891647219658, -0.010700736194849014, -0.009676268324255943, -0.01690703071653843, -0.00577337434515357, 0.04481222480535507, 0.002534732688218355, 0.002310010604560375, 0.00709196412935853, 0.0010905630188062787, 0.042855821549892426, 0.007144839968532324, 0.020714087411761284, -0.019299659878015518, -0.012372933328151703, 0.014977064915001392, -9.728317672852427e-05, -0.006688786204904318, -0.013410620391368866, -0.0036484289448708296, 0.035294584929943085, -0.039974089711904526, 0.0184007715433836, -0.028103478252887726, 0.0001773197582224384, -0.007686816621571779, -0.02428320236504078, -0.019947389140725136, -0.011850784532725811, 